  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes strategies for making stretchable bioelectronic devices and illustrates a hydrogel-based electrode concept for interfacing with soft tissue. At the top, two broad design routes are contrasted: a structural engineering approach, where rigid electronic “islands” are linked by stretchable bridges, and a materials engineering approach, where the electronic component itself is intrinsically stretchable.\n\nA larger comparison then shows common stretchable layouts—serpentine traces, arcs, kirigami cuts, wrinkles, 3D helices, and textile-like structures—being pulled into elongated shapes. A bar scale beneath them compares their maximum stretchability, indicating that these architectures can differ widely, from modest extension to well over 200%, with kirigami and some textile-like designs appearing among the most extensible.\n\nThe lower half shifts to electrode–tissue contact. A conventional electrode is contrasted with a conductive hydrogel coating that appears to create a softer, more conformal interface with mucosa while supporting charge transport through electrons and ions. The schematic highlights polymer chains, covalent bonds, and mobile charges within the hydrogel. The final set of images suggests changes in mechanical behavior during swelling: an initially ductile state, a swollen but fragile state, and a “swell toughening” state where the network appears reinforced and more robust.",
